KPM METRICS DEMYSTIFIED
O-RAN FlexRIC / OAI gNB — All 20 Implemented Metrics
================================================================================


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
THROUGHPUT METRICS  (RLC layer, per UE per DRB)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

DRB.UEThpDl
  What it is:   Downlink throughput for a single UE on one Data Radio Bearer,
                measured at the RLC layer. Computed as the number of bytes
                successfully transmitted to the UE over the air in one
                granularity period, converted to kilobits per second.
  Formula:      delta(txpdu_bytes) × 8 / gran_period_ms  [kbps]
  Used for:     Detecting per-UE DL congestion, validating slicing/scheduling
                fairness, triggering QoS enforcement actions from an xApp.
                A drop here signals poor channel quality, PRB starvation, or
                backhaul bottleneck.

DRB.UEThpUl
  What it is:   Uplink throughput for a single UE on one DRB, measured at
                the RLC layer (bytes received from the UE over the air).
  Formula:      delta(rxpdu_bytes) × 8 / gran_period_ms  [kbps]
  Used for:     Monitoring UL traffic load per UE, detecting UL interference
                or power-limited UEs, UL scheduling optimization.


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PRB USAGE METRICS  (MAC layer, cell-wide)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

RRU.PrbTotDl
  What it is:   Total number of Physical Resource Blocks scheduled in the
                downlink during one granularity period. Spec defines this as
                a percentage of available PRBs, but OAI reports raw PRB count.
  Formula:      delta(total_rbs_dl)  [PRBs]  (spec: / available_PRBs × 100)
  Used for:     Cell-level DL load monitoring. High values indicate the cell
                is heavily loaded. Used by xApps to trigger handovers, adjust
                slicing allocations, or flag congestion.

RRU.PrbTotUl
  What it is:   Total number of Physical Resource Blocks scheduled in the
                uplink during one granularity period.
  Formula:      delta(total_rbs_ul)  [PRBs]  (spec: / available_PRBs × 100)
  Used for:     Cell-level UL load monitoring. Complements DRB.UEThpUl to
                distinguish between few high-rate UEs vs many low-rate UEs
                consuming the same total PRBs.


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PDCP DATA VOLUME METRICS  (PDCP layer, per UE per DRB)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

DRB.PdcpSduVolumeDL
  What it is:   Volume of PDCP SDU data delivered to the UE on the downlink.
                Measured at the PDCP layer (above RLC), so it counts user-plane
                IP bytes before segmentation.
  Formula:      delta(rxsdu_bytes) × 8 / 1000  [kb]
  Used for:     Accounting and QoS assurance. Compared against DRB.UEThpDl
                to detect PDCP-to-RLC queuing or retransmission overhead.
                Also used for usage-based billing or SLA monitoring.

DRB.PdcpSduVolumeUL
  What it is:   Volume of PDCP SDU data received from the UE on the uplink.
  Formula:      delta(txsdu_bytes) × 8 / 1000  [kb]
  Used for:     Same as DL volume but for uplink. Useful for detecting
                asymmetric traffic patterns or UL-heavy applications.


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
TB / HARQ METRICS  (MAC layer, per UE)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Background: Each downlink or uplink packet is broken into Transport Blocks (TBs).
The MAC HARQ process attempts to transmit each TB up to 8 rounds. rounds[0] is
the first (initial) attempt; rounds[1] is a first retransmission, and so on.
If the TB still fails after all rounds, it is counted as an error.

TB.TotNbrDlInitial
  What it is:   Total number of DL TBs transmitted for the first time
                (initial transmissions only, no retransmissions) in one
                granularity period.
  Formula:      delta(dl.rounds[0])  [count]
  Used for:     Baseline DL traffic volume. Denominator for computing DL BLER
                (Block Error Rate = TB.IntialErrNbrDl / TB.TotNbrDlInitial).
                Rising value confirms the scheduler is actively serving the UE.

TB.IntialErrNbrDl
  What it is:   Number of DL TBs that were not decoded correctly on the first
                attempt and required a first retransmission (HARQ round 1).
  Formula:      delta(dl.rounds[1])  [count]
  Used for:     Direct measure of DL initial BLER. High values indicate poor
                channel quality, interference, or misconfigured MCS. Drives
                link adaptation and beam management decisions.

TB.TotNbrDl.sum
  What it is:   Total number of DL TBs across all HARQ rounds (initial +
                all retransmissions combined). Reflects total MAC-layer
                transmission effort including retransmissions.
  Formula:      sum of delta(dl.rounds[0..7])  [count]
  Used for:     Overhead quantification. The difference
                (TB.TotNbrDl.sum - TB.TotNbrDlInitial) is the total number
                of DL retransmissions, which directly translates to wasted
                air-time and latency.

TB.ResidualErrNbrDl
  What it is:   Number of DL TBs that failed decoding after all HARQ rounds —
                i.e., TBs permanently lost at the MAC layer.
  Formula:      delta(dl.errors)  [count]
  Used for:     Worst-case DL reliability indicator. Non-zero values mean data
                was dropped at MAC and must be recovered by RLC ARQ or TCP
                retransmission, causing latency spikes. Signals severe channel
                degradation or interference.

TB.TotNbrUlInit
  What it is:   Total number of UL TBs received for the first time (initial
                transmissions) from the UE in one granularity period.
  Formula:      delta(ul.rounds[0])  [count]
  Used for:     UL traffic volume baseline. Used as denominator for UL BLER.
                A UE that is power-limited or far from the gNB will have low
                TB.TotNbrUlInit relative to PRB allocation.

TB.ErrNbrUlInitial
  What it is:   Number of UL TBs not decoded correctly on the first attempt,
                requiring a retransmission request (NACK) back to the UE.
  Formula:      delta(ul.rounds[1])  [count]
  Used for:     UL initial BLER. High values indicate UL interference, weak
                UE signal, or poor UL power control. Drives UL link adaptation
                and UL scheduling decisions.

TB.TotNbrUl.sum
  What it is:   Total number of UL TBs across all HARQ rounds. Captures both
                successful first transmissions and all retransmissions from UEs.
  Formula:      sum of delta(ul.rounds[0..7])  [count]
  Used for:     UL retransmission overhead. The difference
                (TB.TotNbrUl.sum - TB.TotNbrUlInit) equals total UL HARQ
                retransmissions, reflecting wasted UL PRBs and UE battery drain.

TB.ResidualErrNbrUl
  What it is:   Number of UL TBs permanently lost after all HARQ rounds —
                packets the gNB could not decode despite all retransmission
                attempts.
  Formula:      delta(ul.errors)  [count]
  Used for:     Worst-case UL reliability. Non-zero values indicate critical
                UL channel problems. Results in RLC/TCP-level recovery and
                significant latency increase for the UE.


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PACKET DROP / LOSS METRICS  (RLC layer, per UE per DRB)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

DRB.RlcPacketDropRateDl
  What it is:   Fraction of DL RLC packets (SDUs) that were discarded in the
                gNB-DU due to buffer overflow, congestion, or timer expiry —
                before they could be transmitted over the air.
                Note: our implementation uses x1000 (per mille); spec §5.1.3.2.2
                defines x1,000,000 (per micro). Values are still directionally
                correct.
  Formula:      1000 × delta(txpdu_dd_pkts) / delta(txpdu_pkts)  [per mille]
  Used for:     DL buffer congestion detection. A rising value means the gNB
                RLC buffer is overwhelmed — typically because the scheduler
                cannot keep up with incoming data from the core network.
                Triggers xApp actions like DL rate limiting or PRB reallocation.

DRB.PacketLossRateUl
  What it is:   Fraction of UL RLC packets received from the UE that were
                discarded at the gNB-DU (e.g., duplicate, out-of-window, or
                reassembly failure).
  Formula:      1000 × delta(rxpdu_dd_pkts) / delta(rxpdu_pkts)  [per mille]
  Used for:     UL loss detection beyond the HARQ layer. Complements
                TB.ResidualErrNbrUl — MAC-level losses become RLC-level losses
                and appear here. Useful for diagnosing UL radio quality issues
                that persist after HARQ exhaustion.

DRB.RlcSduLatencyDl
  What it is:   Current head-of-line (HOL) waiting time for the oldest DL
                SDU sitting in the RLC transmission buffer, i.e., how long
                the front packet has been waiting to be scheduled.
  Formula:      txsdu_wt_us  [us]  (instantaneous snapshot, not an average)
  Used for:     DL latency monitoring for delay-sensitive services (URLLC,
                video). A high value means packets are stuck in the buffer —
                caused by insufficient PRB allocation, deep HARQ retransmissions,
                or scheduler deprioritization. Directly relevant for E2E
                latency SLA enforcement.


────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RRC CONNECTION METRICS  (MAC UE list, cell-wide)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

RRC.ConnMean
  What it is:   Mean number of UEs in RRC_CONNECTED state during the
                granularity period. In our implementation, this is a snapshot
                count of the active UE list at the time of reporting (not a
                true arithmetic mean over the interval).
  Formula:      count of non-null entries in RC.nrmac[0]->UE_info.list  [UEs]
  Used for:     Cell load monitoring at the connection level. An xApp uses
                this to track how many UEs are actively attached, detect
                sudden drops (UE disconnection event), or trigger admission
                control when the cell is full. Baseline for capacity planning.

RRC.ConnMax
  What it is:   Maximum number of UEs simultaneously in RRC_CONNECTED state
                observed within the granularity period. In our implementation,
                since reporting is instantaneous, ConnMax equals ConnMean
                within the same interval; the difference appears across
                consecutive intervals.
  Formula:      max snapshot count over gran_period  [UEs]
  Used for:     Peak load detection. Where ConnMean shows the average load,
                ConnMax catches transient spikes — e.g., a burst of UEs
                connecting simultaneously. Used for dimensioning and for
                setting thresholds in anomaly detection models.


